\pard\qc\b\fs36 Secure Deduplication with Ownership Verification and Anomaly Detection\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\i Technical Report\i0\par
\pard\qc February 12, 2026\par
\pard\par
\pard\b Abstract\b0\par
\pard This report documents a secure deduplication prototype that combines content chunking, chunk-level hashing, proof-of-ownership (PoW), and behavior-based attack detection. The system supports duplicate-safe uploads, protects against ownership fraud, and applies runtime policy actions (ALLOW, RATE_LIMIT, BLOCK) based on a learned risk score. In the current run configuration, the deployed detection mode is unsupervised using Isolation Forest and One-Class SVM with weighted voting.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 1. Problem Statement and Objectives\b0\par
\pard Cloud deduplication reduces storage by reusing identical chunks, but introduces security risks: (1) adversaries can probe for existing content, (2) unauthorized clients can claim duplicate ownership, and (3) high-rate duplicate queries can drive denial-of-service behavior. The project objectives are:\par
\pard 1) Preserve storage efficiency through chunk-level deduplication.\par
\pard 2) Verify duplicate ownership before reference reuse.\par
\pard 3) Detect anomalous client behavior and enforce policy controls in real time.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 2. System Architecture\b0\par
\pard\b API Layer\b0: FastAPI endpoints implement /upload, /pow/challenge, /pow/verify, and /health.\par
\pard\b Data Path\b0: Uploaded bytes are chunked (FastCDC or fixed-size fallback), each chunk is hashed using SHA-256, dedup index is queried, then new chunks are stored in MinIO or local disk.\par
\pard\b Index Layer\b0: Reference counts are maintained in Redis, with in-memory fallback when Redis is unavailable.\par
\pard\b Telemetry Layer\b0: Request events and feature snapshots are persisted to SQLite and CSV for training/evaluation.\par
\pard\b Detection Layer\b0: Runtime detector prefers supervised artifacts when available; otherwise it executes unsupervised weighted anomaly voting.\par
\pard\b Policy Layer\b0: Risk score thresholds map to ALLOW, RATE_LIMIT, or BLOCK with cooldown enforcement.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 3. Mathematical Formulation\b0\par
\pard\b 3.1 Chunk Hashing\b0\par
\pard For each chunk c_i, the content identifier is:\par
\pard\qc H_i = SHA256(c_i)\par
\pard\ql Deduplication checks existence of H_i in the chunk index.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 3.2 Proof-of-Ownership (PoW)\b0\par
\pard For a duplicate chunk, server issues challenge tuple (n, o, l) where n is nonce, o is byte offset, l is proof length. Client must return:\par
\pard\qc P = SHA256(n || c[o:o+l])\par
\pard\ql Verification succeeds iff P equals server-computed value for the stored chunk. Challenge IDs are one-time-use and time-bounded.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 3.3 Behavioral Features\b0\par
\pard Let N be total events for a client session. Key engineered features include:\par
\pard requests_per_minute = |events in last 60s|\par
\pard duplicate_ratio = N_pow / N\par
\pard pow_attempt_rate = N_pow / N\par
\pard hash_diversity = N_unique_hashes / N\par
\pard upload_to_query_ratio = N_upload_chunk / max(1, N_hash_query)\par
\pard burst_score = N_60s / max(1, N_300s)\par
\pard session_duration = t_last - t_first\par
\pard cross_user_hash_overlap = |client_hashes intersect other_clients_hashes|\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 3.4 Unsupervised Risk Aggregation\b0\par
\pard For each model m, anomaly flag a_m belongs to {0,1}. Weighted risk is:\par
\pard\qc risk = (sum(w_m * a_m)) / (sum(w_m))\par
\pard\ql Runtime defaults from metadata:\par
\pard w_IF = 0.25, w_OCSVM = 0.20, w_DAE = 0.25, w_LSTM = 0.30\par
\pard Current active models: Isolation Forest and One-Class SVM.\par
\pard Decision rule:\par
\pard anomaly = 1 if risk >= tau, else 0, with tau = 0.50\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 3.5 Policy Mapping\b0\par
\pard Let r be risk score. Then:\par
\pard if r >= 0.80 -> BLOCK (HTTP 403)\par
\pard else if r >= 0.55 -> RATE_LIMIT (HTTP 429)\par
\pard else -> ALLOW (HTTP 200)\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 4. Models and Algorithms\b0\par
\pard\b 4.1 Supervised Candidate Set (when labels are trainable)\b0\par
\pard 1) HistGradientBoostingClassifier\par
\pard 2) RandomForestClassifier\par
\pard 3) LogisticRegression\par
\pard Model selection uses GridSearchCV with StratifiedKFold and scoring metric f1_macro.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 4.2 Unsupervised Stack\b0\par
\pard 1) Isolation Forest (contamination default 0.05, estimators default 300)\par
\pard 2) One-Class SVM (RBF kernel, nu default 0.05)\par
\pard 3) Optional Dense Autoencoder (if TensorFlow installed)\par
\pard 4) Optional LSTM Autoencoder (sequence length default 10, if TensorFlow installed)\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 4.3 Upload + Dedup + PoW Algorithm (runtime)\b0\par
\pard\f1
Algorithm UploadAndVerify(file, client):\line
  chunks <- Chunk(file)\line
  for each chunk in chunks:\line
    h <- SHA256(chunk)\line
    if h exists in dedup index:\line
      require valid PoW for (client, h)\line
      if no valid proof: return 409 with challenge\line
  for each chunk in chunks:\line
    if h not exists: store chunk and create ref_count=1\line
    else: increment ref_count\line
  features <- ExtractFeatures(client_logs)\line
  detect <- Detect(features)\line
  policy <- DecidePolicy(detect.risk)\line
  persist features + label + policy\line
  return success payload\f0\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 5. Evaluation Metrics\b0\par
\pard Binary anomaly metrics (normal vs anomaly):\par
\pard Precision = TP / (TP + FP)\par
\pard Recall = TP / (TP + FN)\par
\pard F1 = 2 * Precision * Recall / (Precision + Recall)\par
\pard PR-AUC = area under precision-recall curve\par
\pard Confusion matrix tracks [TN, FP; FN, TP].\par
\pard\par
\pard For multiclass supervised runs, macro-F1 and one-vs-rest PR-AUC are reported.\par
\pard\par
