\pard\b 6.4 Metric Availability Note\b0\par
\pard Binary PR-AUC and F1 are marked unavailable in this evaluation because the provided ground truth mapped to a single binary class (all non-normal labels), making normal-vs-anomaly discrimination metrics non-informative for that split.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 7. Security and Engineering Assessment\b0\par
\pard\b Strengths\b0\par
\pard 1) PoW challenge-response blocks unauthorized duplicate claims.\par
\pard 2) Stateful one-time challenge usage reduces replay risk.\par
\pard 3) Risk-based policy control enables adaptive throttling/blocking.\par
\pard 4) Redis/MinIO fallbacks improve local deploy resilience.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b Current Limitations\b0\par
\pard 1) Limited class balance for binary anomaly evaluation in current dataset snapshot.\par
\pard 2) Optional deep models are inactive unless TensorFlow artifacts are present.\par
\pard 3) Fixed thresholds may require environment-specific calibration.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 8. Recommended Next Steps\b0\par
\pard 1) Collect and retain explicitly normal traffic windows to stabilize binary evaluation metrics.\par
\pard 2) Run supervised mode on relabeled balanced data and compare macro-F1 vs unsupervised baseline.\par
\pard 3) Enable dense/LSTM autoencoders and tune weighted ensemble thresholds using validation curves.\par
\pard 4) Add per-attack confusion matrices and calibration plots to periodic evaluation reports.\par
\pard\par
\pard\b 9. Conclusion\b0\par
\pard The implemented system is a practical secure dedup pipeline that ties storage efficiency to ownership verification and behavior-aware defense. The architecture already supports production-relevant controls (PoW, risk scoring, and policy cooldowns), and can be further improved through better class-balanced evaluation data and calibrated thresholding.\par
\pard\par
